What I Learned
I learned about HTML elements on freeCodeCamp and took notes on the important details.
I learned that:

<h1> creates a main heading.
<img src="image.png"> adds an image to a webpage.
<a href="https://example.com">Link text</a> creates a link.
<ol> creates an ordered list, and <ul> creates an unordered list.
The id attribute can identify an element and help create links to parts of a page.
You can make an image clickable by placing an <img> element inside an <a> element.
Using href="#" creates a placeholder link that doesn’t navigate to another webpage.

Process
First, I added a heading to my webpage:

          
            
            
          
          <h1>Red Pandas</h1>
      Next, I added an image. I placed the image file in the img folder and used an <img> element to display it:

          
            
            
          
          <img src="img/redpanda.jpg" alt="A red panda">
      Then, I added a subheading and started an unordered list:

          
            
            
          
          <h3>Facts:</h3>
<ul>
  <li>They're not actually pandas and aren't related to giant pandas.</li>
  <li>They have an internet browser named after them.</li>
  <li>They are an endangered species.</li>
  <li>They can run up to 24 mph.</li>
</ul>
      To link the words “internet browser named after them” to Mozilla, I placed an anchor element around those words:

          
            
            
          
          <li>They have an <a href="https://www.mozilla.org">internet browser named after them</a>.</li>
      Finally, I added a link to the Wikipedia page about red pandas:

          
            
            
          
          <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_panda">Learn More</a>
      Mistakes
One mistake I made on freeCodeCamp was confusing the letter l with the number 1. I typed <u1> instead of <ul>. After seeing the error several times, I looked more closely, found the mistake, and corrected it.
Incorrect code:

          
            
            
          
          <u1>
  <li>Milk</li>
  <li>Cheese</li>
</u1>
      Correct code:

          
            
            
          
          <ul>
  <li>Milk</li>
  <li>Cheese</li>
</ul>
      I also tried to indent a list item by adding spaces before its code. That changed the way the code looked, but it did not create a nested list. After asking Vivian on Slack, I learned that I needed to put another list inside a list item.

          
            
            
          
          <ul>
  <li>Regular item
    <ul>
      <li>Indented item</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
</ul>
      I tested the code in JSBin and saw that the nested item appeared as a sub-list.
